{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Speak to Aaron, any man of your seed</b> — Here it does not say \"Speak to Aaron and to his sons\" as it says in all the other sections, and the reason is that there He commanded in the sections of the offerings: \"This is the law of the burnt offering\" (Leviticus 6:2) and \"This is the law of the sin offering\" (ibid. verse 18) and the guilt offering and similar matters. But here, if it had said \"Speak to Aaron and to his sons,\" it would have been appropriate to say \"any man of you, throughout your generations,\" and He would not have intended to warn Aaron himself concerning the law of blemishes, for Aaron is holy to HaShem, entirely whole, and no blemish will be found in him. Rather, He warns him about his seed, that he must teach them and warn them throughout their generations. And in the section of impurity nearby (Leviticus 22:2), \"Speak to Aaron and to his sons, and they shall separate themselves from the holy things of the children of Israel\" — for it is possible that even Aaron might experience the impurity of contact with a corpse or a creeping thing. But when He came to mention the tzara'at and the zav, He reverted and said (ibid. verse 4), \"Any man of the seed of Aaron,\" for this will not occur in his own person, for he is the angel of YHWH of Hosts. And He said at the end of the section (Leviticus 21:24), \"And Moses spoke to Aaron and to his sons\" — because Moses wished to warn his sons as well, that they should receive the instruction from him, and he said the same to the children of Israel, to warn the court of Israel concerning this. And in Targum Yonatan (section 3, law 5) I found: \"Every man\" — what does this teach? Since it is stated \"the seed of Aaron,\" I have only his seed; Aaron himself, from where do I learn? The verse teaches, saying \"for any man in whom there is a blemish\" (Leviticus 21:18). And likewise they taught there (chapter 4, mishnah 1): \"the seed of Aaron\" — I have only his seed; Aaron himself, from where do I learn? The verse teaches, saying \"and he is a metzora or a zav\" (Leviticus 22:4). And the meaning of \"Aaron himself\" is the High Priest who serves in his stead, from among his sons, for since the Torah permitted the mourning rites to a High Priest, perhaps it would permit to him a blemish and tzara'at, and it was necessary to include him. Or it may hint at the law of Aaron himself, for the Torah does not base its warnings upon a miracle, but it concealed the matter concerning Aaron for his honor, and to hint to us that such a thing would not befall him."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The citation 'בת\"כ (פרשה ג ה)' refers to the Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael (or possibly Sifra); Ramban designates it by parashah and halakhah (or mishnah) numbers. The subsequent citation 'פרק ד א' refers to Sifra, Naso, chapter 4, mishnah 1."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:38.961936690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}